{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,320p' /app/agent-output/S16-self-host-upgrade-trap/report.md\"","aggregated_output":"# S16 — The Self-Host Upgrade Trap\n\n## 1. Structural root cause\n\nThis was a self-upgrade of the running control plane. The web session was hosted by a\nNode process whose code came from the global\n`node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh/` tree. The agent's tool worker was running inside that\nhost process. The command it launched was therefore not an independent maintenance\nprocess: it was a child/worker operation controlled by the very dsh installation that\n`npm install -g` was removing and replacing.\n\nThe log shows npm progressing through cleanup/removal, fetching rc.1, and\n`linkStuff`. Once npm started changing the live package tree, the dsh host/session lost\nthe code it was executing or otherwise became unloadable. The first user-visible death\nwas the dsh web session/GUI (connection lost). That kills the worker/control channel\nbefore the npm child can report its final status. Consequently the tool call has no\ndurable result; its success or failure is not evidence that the install completed.\n\nThis is structural, not a flaky network or browser failure: the process issuing the\nupgrade depends on the files being replaced. A running process may retain some already\nloaded code, but it cannot provide a reliable upgrade transaction while its own module\ntree and process supervision are being torn down.\n\n## 2. State after the interrupted install\n\nThe package directory is not the command. On this Windows installation, npm's global\ninstall also creates the command shims in `%APPDATA%\\\\npm`. The before/after evidence\nshows that after the interruption:\n\n- rc.1 package content existed in `node_modules\\\\@deepseek-ai\\\\dsh`, but the tree was\n  partial/non-standard and had also been manually swapped in places;\n- `dsh` and `dsh.cmd` were missing, while `dsh.ps1` was stale and still pointed into\n  the old tree;\n- an old backup directory remained.\n\nThus PowerShell could not resolve `dsh` at all, even though files bearing rc.1 content\nwere present. The missing generated shims, especially `dsh.cmd` and the main `dsh`\nentrypoint, severed the PATH-facing command.\n\nHand-swapping or patching package directories would compound the problem. It does not\nrecreate npm's bin/shim outputs or complete the package-manager lifecycle, and it can\nleave a mixture of alpha, rc, stale paths, and incomplete dependencies that npm no\nlonger recognizes as a standard install. It also risks changing files while another\ndsh process is using them. The package directory's presence is therefore not a safe\nrepair criterion.\n\n## 3. Repair actually applied\n\nAn independent agent CLI, run outside dsh, re-ran the formal registry install:\n\n```text\nnpm install -g @deepseek-ai/dsh@0.1.2-rc.1\n```\n\nThat completed with an external process no longer hosted by the package being replaced.\nThe formal install rebuilt the package installation and regenerated all three shims:\n`dsh`, `dsh.cmd`, and `dsh.ps1`. The direct verification recorded in the repair notes\nwas:\n\n```text\ndsh --version  ->  0.1.2-rc.1\n```\n\nThe source checkout used for host-source reference was also aligned from the\n`dsh-v0.1.2-alpha.5` tag to `dsh-v0.1.2-rc.1`, with no local-modification conflict.\nThat alignment is separate from regenerating the global command, but keeps the source\nreference consistent with the installed release.\n\n## 4. Protocol the agent should have followed\n\nThe agent should have recognized that the requested package is its own runtime and that\nthe current session is not an external administrator shell. It should not execute the\nglobal dsh install from that session at all. It should explain the self-hosting hazard\nand hand the user this externally executed procedure:\n\n1. From a separate shell or external agent CLI, stop/exit the dsh web host and end the\n   dsh session. Ensure no dsh host process is still running before npm starts. Closing\n   the browser tab alone is not sufficient if the host process remains alive.\n2. In that independent shell, run the exact formal global registry install:\n\n   ```text\n   npm install -g @deepseek-ai/dsh@0.1.2-rc.1\n   ```\n\n   The `-g` targets the global installation and the explicit package version selects\n   rc.1; using npm's formal install is what reinstalls the package and generates its\n   platform command shims. Do not replace the directory by hand.\n3. Verify the command and version, including that the shims resolve from the expected\n   global npm bin directory. At minimum run `dsh --version` and require\n   `0.1.2-rc.1`.\n4. If the source checkout is used for host-source reference, align it to\n   `dsh-v0.1.2-rc.1` after checking for local modifications.\n5. Start the web profile with `dsh --profile web`, hard-refresh the browser, and smoke\n   test the GUI, session, tools, and plugins.\n\nThe established upgrade rules reflected by the repair are to use the package manager's\nformal, exact-version install, avoid manual tree surgery, keep the source checkout on\nthe matching release tag, and verify the installed version and runtime afterward. The\nincident-specific addition is the execution boundary: because dsh is self-hosting,\nthe host must be stopped before npm runs and the install must be performed by a\nseparate external process. That boundary is the part the in-session agent failed to\napply.\n\n## 5. Prevention and post-upgrade checklist\n\nAn agent-side guard should inspect any package-manager command before execution. If it\ntargets `@deepseek-ai/dsh` globally (install, update, uninstall, or an equivalent\ndirectory replacement), compare it with the current process/module path and host\nidentity. If the target is the current dsh runtime, the guard must block the tool call,\nstate that the control plane would be replacing itself, and emit the external procedure\nabove. A confirmation inside the same session cannot make this safe; the required\ncondition is an independent process after the host has stopped.\n\nFor this alpha.5 to rc.1 upgrade, the post-upgrade checklist should cover:\n\n- `dsh --version` reports exactly `0.1.2-rc.1`;\n- `dsh`, `dsh.cmd`, and `dsh.ps1` all exist in the global npm bin directory and resolve\n  to the current installation;\n- the global package tree is a standard npm install, not a hand-swapped or mixed tree;\n- the source checkout, if used, is on `dsh-v0.1.2-rc.1` and has no unintended local\n  modifications;\n- `dsh --profile web` starts, the browser is hard-refreshed, and the GUI reconnects;\n- a real session can execute tools and the whale, progress, and other expected plugins\n  load;\n- stale `dsh-old-*` backup directories are cleaned up only after the new install and\n  runtime have been confirmed.\n\nThe repair notes report that the alpha.5-to-rc.1 diff contains 252 files, all package\nversion bumps, with no API or feature changes. Therefore migrated plugins do not need\nre-migration; they still need the runtime smoke test above to catch installation,\nshim, or startup problems.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
